<p>In property management, there are two fundamentally different approaches to maintenance: reactive and proactive. Reactive maintenance means dealing with problems when they break. Proactive maintenance means preventing them from breaking in the first place. The financial case for the proactive approach is overwhelming, yet a surprising number of landlords and homeowners still default to the "if it isn't broken, don't fix it" mentality — usually because planned maintenance feels like an unnecessary cost until the moment it becomes an emergency repair that costs three times as much.</p>

<p>At Ark Housing, proactive property maintenance is central to how we manage every property in our portfolio. In this article, we break down the key maintenance areas that every property owner should monitor regularly and explain why the cost of prevention is always lower than the cost of cure.</p>

<h2>1. The True Cost of Reactive vs Proactive Maintenance</h2>

<p>To understand why proactive maintenance makes financial sense, consider a simple example. A boiler that is serviced annually costs around £80–£120 per year for the service. A boiler that is never serviced and eventually breaks down in the middle of January costs anywhere from £500 for a repair to £3,000–£5,000 for a full replacement — plus the potential cost of emergency call-out fees, temporary heating for tenants, and any legal liability for failing to maintain heating and hot water. The maths is straightforward.</p>

<p>The principle scales across every aspect of property maintenance. A blocked gutter that costs £80 to clear can lead to water ingress that causes £5,000 worth of internal damage if left unattended for a season. A slow roof leak that costs a few hundred pounds to fix early can result in ceiling collapses, structural damage, and mould remediation bills running into tens of thousands if ignored for years. In property, the cost of doing nothing is almost always higher than the cost of doing something.</p>

