<h2>2. Annual Boiler Servicing and Heating Systems</h2>

<p>For landlords, gas appliance safety is not just a matter of financial prudence — it's a legal requirement. The Gas Safety (Installation and Use) Regulations 1998 require all gas appliances in rental properties to be inspected and certified by a Gas Safe registered engineer every 12 months. The resulting Gas Safety Certificate must be provided to tenants at the start of their tenancy and within 28 days of each annual check. Failure to comply can result in criminal prosecution, unlimited fines, and prohibition notices.</p>

<p>Beyond compliance, an annual boiler service gives the engineer the opportunity to identify and address minor issues — corroded valves, worn seals, low pressure — before they escalate into expensive failures. Engineers also check carbon monoxide levels, flue integrity, and ventilation, which has direct implications for tenant safety. For owner-occupiers without the legal obligation, annual servicing remains the single best investment in boiler longevity; modern boilers can last 15+ years with proper care and fail within 8–10 without it.</p>

<h2>3. Roof and Guttering Inspections</h2>

<p>Your roof is your property's primary defence against the British weather. Yet many property owners go years without inspecting it, assuming that if they can't see water coming through the ceiling, everything must be fine. In reality, slow leaks can progress for months or years beneath roof felt and within wall cavities before they become visible internally — by which point the damage is already extensive.</p>

<p>Commission a visual roof inspection every two to three years, and additionally after any significant storm. A roofer can spot cracked or slipped tiles, failing flashings around chimneys and dormers, and deteriorating ridge pointing before any of these cause internal damage. Guttering should be cleaned at least once a year — ideally in late autumn after the leaves have fallen — to prevent blockages that cause water to overflow and saturate the fascias, soffits, and walls beneath.</p>

<h2>4. Electrical Safety</h2>

<p>Electrical faults cause more than 20,000 house fires in the UK every year. For landlords in the private rented sector, Electrical Installation Condition Reports (EICRs) are now mandatory — all rental properties must have a valid EICR carried out by a qualified electrician every five years, with a copy provided to tenants. Properties that fail the inspection must have remedial work completed within 28 days.</p>

<p>For owner-occupiers, there's no legal obligation, but commissioning an EICR if you've purchased an older property, or if your wiring hasn't been inspected for a decade or more, is strongly advisable. Rewiring, if required, is expensive — typically £3,000–£10,000 depending on property size — but an electrical fire is catastrophically more so, both financially and in terms of personal safety.</p>

<img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1504307651254-35680f356dfd?auto=format&fit=crop&q=80&w=1200" alt="Electrical maintenance" style="width:100%; border-radius:16px; margin:32px 0; object-fit:cover; height:380px;">

<h2>5. Damp and Mould Prevention</h2>

<p>Damp and mould are among the most common — and most damaging — issues in UK residential property. They affect occupant health, reduce property values, cause structural deterioration, and have become an increasingly prominent legal issue for landlords following high-profile cases of tenant illness linked to mould exposure. The Homes (Fitness for Human Habitation) Act 2018 gives tenants the right to take legal action against landlords who fail to address serious damp and mould issues.</p>

<p>Prevention starts with understanding the causes. Rising damp comes from failed damp proof courses and requires specialist treatment. Penetrating damp comes from faulty external elements — leaking roofs, failing render, defective pointing — and is fixed at the source. Condensation damp, the most common type, results from insufficient ventilation and heating within the property. Addressing it requires ensuring adequate ventilation (particularly in kitchens and bathrooms), maintaining consistent heating, and sometimes installing mechanical ventilation systems in high-risk areas.</p>

<h2>6. Creating a Proactive Maintenance Schedule</h2>

<p>The most effective approach to property maintenance is a structured, calendar-based schedule that ensures all key elements are checked and serviced at the appropriate intervals. A basic framework for a rental property might look like this:</p>

<p><strong>Annually:</strong> Gas safety check and boiler service, gutter cleaning, smoke and carbon monoxide detector testing, external paintwork and joinery inspection.</p>

<p><strong>Every 2–3 years:</strong> Roof inspection, external re-pointing where required, PAT testing of any supplied electrical appliances, redecorating where needed.</p>

<p><strong>Every 5 years:</strong> Electrical Installation Condition Report, full internal redecoration, review of kitchen and bathroom condition, EPC assessment.</p>

<p>Building these costs into your property budget — rather than treating them as unexpected expenses — transforms maintenance from a source of stress and financial surprise into a managed, predictable part of property ownership. At Ark Housing, we handle all maintenance scheduling and management for the properties in our portfolio, ensuring every property we manage remains fully compliant, well-maintained, and attractive to tenants.</p>

<h2>Final Thoughts</h2>

<p>Proactive maintenance is not a cost — it's an investment. It protects the value of your asset, ensures legal compliance, reduces the risk of emergency expenses, and demonstrates to your tenants that you take their home seriously. The landlords who thrive long-term are those who treat their properties as the valuable assets they are and manage them with the same diligence they'd apply to any other investment.</p>

